WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.447
Here's a server that does ten milliseconds of work per request, on average.

00:00:05.697 --> 00:00:11.719
Send it eighty requests a second, and it's working eighty percent of the time: it's eighty percent busy.

00:00:11.769 --> 00:00:16.603
A request then takes fifty milliseconds on average, from arrival to reply.

00:00:16.853 --> 00:00:22.202
Send it ninety requests a second, an eighth more traffic, and it's ninety percent busy.

00:00:22.252 --> 00:00:24.855
Requests now take a hundred milliseconds.

00:00:25.105 --> 00:00:28.588
You might expect a little more traffic to mean a little more delay.

00:00:28.638 --> 00:00:30.880
Instead, the response time doubled.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:31.809
Why?

00:00:31.859 --> 00:00:38.373
And even at eighty percent, where do forty of those fifty milliseconds come from, when the work only takes ten?

00:00:42.123 --> 00:00:50.192
A request's response time has two parts: the time the server spends working on it, and the time it spends waiting for the server to be free.

00:00:50.442 --> 00:00:54.875
The work takes ten milliseconds on average, however busy the server is.

00:00:55.125 --> 00:01:01.812
So at eighty percent busy, a request waits forty milliseconds in a queue, four times the average work.

00:01:01.862 --> 00:01:04.138
At ninety percent, it waits ninety.

00:01:04.388 --> 00:01:06.420
The question is really about queues.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:10.073
Why do they get so long, when the server still has time to spare?

00:01:11.623 --> 00:01:17.546
Suppose requests arrived evenly spaced, and every one took exactly ten milliseconds.

00:01:17.796 --> 00:01:27.235
At ninety percent busy, ten milliseconds of work fills ninety percent of the gap between requests, so a new one arrives about every eleven milliseconds.

00:01:27.485 --> 00:01:30.334
It finds the server already done with the last one.

00:01:30.384 --> 00:01:36.365
Nobody ever waits, and every response takes ten milliseconds, even at ninety percent busy.

00:01:37.415 --> 00:01:39.614
Real traffic varies in two ways.

00:01:39.664 --> 00:01:43.687
Requests arrive at random, so sometimes several come close together.

00:01:43.737 --> 00:01:46.656
And some requests need much more work than others.

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:52.436
When a clump arrives, the server can only work on one at a time, and the rest wait in a queue.

00:01:53.686 --> 00:01:55.732
So queues come from variability.

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:57.604
What decides how long they last?

00:01:58.554 --> 00:02:03.444
A queue shrinks only when the server finishes work faster than new work arrives.

00:02:03.694 --> 00:02:09.014
At eighty percent busy, new work keeps arriving at eighty percent of what the server can do.

00:02:09.064 --> 00:02:14.026
So while there's a queue, only the other twenty percent of its effort actually shrinks it.

00:02:14.076 --> 00:02:18.227
Call that twenty percent the spare capacity: it's what clears the queue.

00:02:18.477 --> 00:02:22.712
At ninety percent busy, the spare capacity is only ten percent.

00:02:22.762 --> 00:02:29.574
On average, the same queue takes about twice as long to clear, and more requests pile on while that happens.

00:02:29.624 --> 00:02:33.085
That's the intuition; the exact answer comes next.

00:02:33.335 --> 00:02:36.702
Here's the same random stream of requests at both loads.

00:02:36.752 --> 00:02:40.169
At ninety percent, the queue builds higher and lasts longer.

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:46.405
The fraction of time a server is busy is called its utilization.

00:02:46.655 --> 00:02:51.472
For the simplest case, queueing theory gives the exact average response time.

00:02:51.523 --> 00:03:03.195
The case is one server, requests arriving at random, each unrelated to the others, and work times that vary just as randomly: mostly short, sometimes several times longer.

00:03:03.445 --> 00:03:11.219
The answer is the work time divided by the spare capacity: ten milliseconds divided by one minus the utilization.

00:03:11.469 --> 00:03:14.630
At fifty percent busy, that's twenty milliseconds.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:15.968
At eighty, fifty.

00:03:16.018 --> 00:03:17.641
At ninety, a hundred.

00:03:17.691 --> 00:03:19.489
At ninety-five, two hundred.

00:03:19.739 --> 00:03:24.093
A simulation of twenty million requests at each load lands on the curve.

00:03:25.143 --> 00:03:27.294
It's almost flat at first, then steep.

00:03:27.344 --> 00:03:32.615
From eighty to ninety percent, the spare capacity halves, and the response time doubles.

00:03:32.665 --> 00:03:36.082
From ninety to ninety-five, it halves and doubles again.

00:03:38.032 --> 00:03:42.654
So that's where the forty milliseconds went: not into work, but into waiting.

00:03:42.704 --> 00:03:51.894
A little more traffic halved the spare capacity, from twenty percent to ten, and the response time doubled with it, from fifty milliseconds to a hundred.

00:03:52.144 --> 00:03:55.697
Burstier traffic, or more variable work, makes this worse.

00:03:55.747 --> 00:04:03.703
Bigger clumps of requests, or a few very long ones, build bigger queues, and the same spare capacity takes longer to clear them.

00:04:03.753 --> 00:04:07.295
So they need more spare capacity for the same response time.

00:04:07.545 --> 00:04:10.005
That's why capacity plans leave headroom.

00:04:10.055 --> 00:04:15.818
Decide how slow requests may get, and read a first estimate of the load limit off the curve.

00:04:15.868 --> 00:04:23.692
On this curve, if a request may take five times its work time on average, keep the server at or below eighty percent busy.

00:04:23.742 --> 00:04:31.077
Real systems, with burstier traffic and more than one busy resource, usually need more headroom than this curve says.

00:04:31.327 --> 00:04:35.089
These are averages, and some requests take several times longer.

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:37.501
The slowest requests are the next lesson.

00:04:37.751 --> 00:04:41.292
And never plan to run a server at a hundred percent busy.

00:04:41.342 --> 00:04:45.643
With no spare capacity, nothing clears the queue, and it keeps growing.
